I opened draft PR #1656 to ship the upstream prudynt-t fMP4 cleanup in Thingino.

The underlying prudynt fix is maintainer commit `354b1b4`: it releases the fMP4 worker claim after the last preview disconnects, avoids writing to an undrained main channel, recycles queued frames, and bounds retained NAL capacity. BCL independently verified the old firmware pin fails the regression check and the proposed pin passes all six source-level checks: https://github.com/iibaranov-IG/broadcast-control-lab/actions/runs/34840592749

@alump @Goldstrike75 @bigskookum, if a maintainer can provide a candidate image, your hardware results would close the remaining gap. Please open and close Live View ten times with no RTSP client, sample `VmRSS` every five seconds, then leave it running for 50 minutes and confirm there is no OOM/reboot and that RTSP plus fMP4 still play. The PR includes the exact procedure and does not claim firmware hardware verification yet.

The fix is ready for hardware validation in PR #184: https://github.com/amicalhq/amical/pull/184

@fututukamono, could you please test that build with the Apollo Twin selected, place the microphone or a test tone on input 2 while input 1 is silent, and confirm that the recorded WAV contains the signal and transcription works? If possible, please also repeat once with a mono microphone to confirm the fallback.

The automated reproduction now passes for a silent channel 1 plus active channel 2, maximum device channel expansion, preservation of the strongest channel without attenuation, mono input, and a device that rejects channel expansion. The full desktop test suite also passes. Physical Apollo validation remains the final check.
